  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nNice late summer weather pattern expected through Tuesday across\ncentral and southeast IL after a very active weather pattern 1st\n3 weeks of the month. Weak 1021 mb surface high pressure over nw\nMO into central Iowa and upper MS river valley to settle over IL\nduring tonight and into the mid Ohio river valley on Mon and Mon\nnight while ridging back into central/ne IL. This will continue\nfair wx across the prairie state into Tuesday with dry conditions\nand more comfortable humidity levels. NW winds 5-15 mph and \nscattered diurnally driven cumulus clouds over central and ne \nIL to dissipate after sunset while veil of high clouds over MO \nand central/se Iowa is clipping west central and sw IL. A \ndisturbance over the central plains to keep chances of \nconvection well sw of IL over central and sw MO tonight into \nearly Monday as it tracks se across the MO valley/Ozarks. Patchy\nshallow ground fog may develop during overnight into early Mon \nmorning mainly near river valleys and low lying rural ares. HREF\nonly has 15-30% chance of vsbys getting to 5 miles or less over\nportions of CWA later tonight. HRRR and RAP show very little \nfog formation with drier air in place while NAM and GFSLamp show\nsome patchy fog development mainly near river valleys. \nComfortable night ahead with lows in the mid to upper 50s. Highs\nMonday around 80F with lower 80s southeast IL and sw CWA, with \nscattered cumulus clouds developing by midday giving partly to \nmostly sunny skies. Lows 55-60F Monday night and highs in the \nlow to mid 80s Tuesday. Winds to stay fairly light generally \nunder 10 mph tonight through Tue. \n\nA northern stream upper level shortwave moving over the southern\nCanadian Prairie Provinces next few days, will track se through\nthe northern Great Lakes Region, sending a cold front se through\nIL during Wed afternoon and Wed night. This will likely bring \nour next chance for showers and thunderstorms. Increasing south\nto SW flow ahead of the cold front to bring in warmer and more\nhumid air into central/se IL on Wed. Highs in the mid to upper\n80s Wed and possibly approaching 90F from Springfield sw with\ndewpoints climbing into the upper 60s/lower 70s. A cap \ninitially in place on Wed could break later in the afternoon and\nWed evening allowing thunderstorms to develop in a moderate \ninstability with wind shear environment. Although the better \nupper forcing will be displaced to our north, a few strong to \nmarginally severe storms could develop later Wednesday \nafternoon and evening, posing a threat for damaging wind gusts.\n\nCould see lingering low chances of showers/thunderstorms in se \nIL early Thu, otherwise fair weather with seasonable \ntemperatures to return Thu through at least Friday night as high\npressure settles into the region. Strong mid/upper level ridge \nover the Southwest States through this work week begins to break\ndown/flatten this weekend, and may see disturbance tracking \ninto the midwest during this weekend especially by Sunday. A few\nmodel members shows some convection chances as early as \nSaturday over the IL river valley, while more members wait until\nSunday for convection chances, though GEM and Ecmwf are dry \nthru the weekend. NBM keeps pops low this weekend with 20% pops \nover IL river valley Sat and Sat evening and 20-40% pops \novernight Sat night and Sunday. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day outlook for Aug\n31-Sep 6th has a 35-45% chance of above normal temperatures \nover central/se IL and 33-40% below normal precipitation from \nQuincy to Danville north and trending near normal south.",
